Older versions of this software used Puppeteer where the new version runs totally in Chrome.  For the most part, the new version is so much better. In particular, I get the real-time preview. I can scrub back and forth with no issues. I used to have to write special weird test things, but now I can just write the code that I want to write and display it.

The problem with the new stuff, the media bunny encoder that I'm using now does not do everything that ffmpeg does. In particular, FFM peg knows how to create transparent backgrounds using proress. I'm sure there are other cases but that's got to be the biggest reason to consider ffmpeg.

One of my biggest complaints about the old version was that it was ridiculously slow. The obvious slowdown was encoding each screenshot as a PNG file. (The JPEG switch didn't significantly help) Additionally, I never got FFM peg to encode things as quickly and efficiently as Media Bunny. Does. There might be options? Options I just never found them and I was rate limited upstream by the PNGs. But I think it was worse than that. Ffmpeg was using lots of threads and making everything hot and still running slow.

Let's consider the use cases. I could use my normal setup with Media, Bunny and Chrome to make big videos. Things were the time involved. Matters. But when it comes to these transparent things, I'm thinking of small parts of a project. Things like" stickers" from capcut.

Also, I can combine the two approaches. I can do all the development exactly as is, then when I'm ready to do the final print I could send something to FFM peg via puppeteer or something similar.

I have two major complaints about the ffmpeg version of my current software. One is that it's slow, very slow. I haven't compared it yet, but I think we're disabling a lot of GPU stuff again when we go into the command line mode required to use ffmpeg in this project. Also, there's stuff that's just different in the two versions and it's hard to test unless I really want to put time into maintaining the ffmpeg version that almost never gets used.

Proposal: throw away the TSX version of my current project. Only run in an interactive window. But have an option where puppeteer or similar can run this program instead of running it in Chrome. The results should be very similar, much more similar than what the current TSX program is offering.

Normally the puppeteer script would start this page in a visible window, so it would look a lot like it runs. Now. You have all the same controls. The difference is it puppeteer would add a few useful things to window. Additional APIs like save to FFM peg. The main program would pick up on these and offer additional GUI because it found this API. So for the most part you're watching things like normal, you just have one extra button to save a new way. Or maybe a lot of options cuz FFM peg offers so many.

There would also be a command line option that would just run everything, case you wanted to do this from a script, without pressing any buttons. A minor add-on, not a major bit of architecture, so I assume we'll get that almost for free.

I had considered a preview mode in the puppeteer version. Something much simpler than we have now, just showing the video as it's being rendered. So just a small subset of what we have now. Because we were doing screenshots instead of writing to the canvas at the time, that really hampered my ability to do that. Already does that perfectly. It can render to exactly the size, the number of pixels in a visible canvas to make it look perfect while previewing. But when recording the canvas size is 4k and it gets shrunk just to fit on the screen for the preview, and that's trivial, just normal. CSS styles.

Another advantage of this new approach, we used to be taking a full screenshot. That's a nice thought because I can use HTML and SVG and so many other resources to draw stuff. But, in practice, the older version was always just SVG. Occasionally the debug stuff would be done in HTML but it was never quite done riding. It never quite scaled right. So it should be trivial? To do the preview.

A related advantage, because we're creating a canvas, we can control exactly how it gets exported. For example, if I found a faster compression algorithm. Or maybe I don't want to do compression at all. Is that actually required? I'm just sending things from one process to the next. Is that significant compared to the time we're already putting in? Just copying it out of the GPU every frame? I.e I know there's a cost but how significant is it compared to the related costs. And of course I can use multi-threading now. I can actually send that canvas off to another thread, a normal JavaScript worker. If nothing else, you could do the transmission and one thread while you're building the next frame in the main thread. Or you could have multiple threads if we really need to do difficult compression. Carriage return carriage return line feed.

One of my goals, my long-term goals, is to separate the videos from the video production environment. Environment. Ideally, you'd write a main program with your video, a new get an NPM project, get git. And then you would import this library which includes the GUI and the other things required to produce a video. I haven't done that yet for a number of reasons. For one thing, JavaScript libraries are just a pain to write and maintain. Especially so on a new project where I'm not exactly certain where to draw the lines between the library and the main program. Now this project is a little more mature, so it's reasonable to at least think about the library option. But one thing that's holding me back, is this TSX version. That's basically two separate main programs to support, not just one, harder to pack into a library.

Open questions: can we make some rough guesses about the performance difference between this new hybrid approach with puppets here, but most of my code from this project versus diversion that is all puppeteer? If not, I might have to do some simple tests. Also, is there an obvious better way to send the canvas to the puppeteer node process? I had very few choices when it came to puppeteer and screenshots. And I didn't like any of them. Maybe a faster third-party library? Maybe just send it all raw, FFM peg can handle raw RGBA four bites per color dumps on its own.

Fyi, I have another version of this software which is all web-based and uses FFM peg built into wasm. Impressive that I can do it, but still a hack. Still another fork of the software so I can't make improvements the library in just one place. It was really good for small things at all. Fidden memory at once. But as hard as I pushed you just couldn't get around that limitation.

I would love to have one normal npm library for this. Normal semantic versioning. I can constantly add small improvements that everyone gets. But it would also be easy to make breaking changes. That's a real problem in the current setup where all the videos are all in one big project. I think semantic versioning would solve so many of my problems.

One of the reasons I want a main program in a library, is to deal with big top level settings like the size of the output in pixels. I'm sure I could do that somehow in my program, in the current program, but there was no clean way to do it. If I had one video per project, the library would default to the same things, but it would be very easy to change from the main program to whatever you wanted.
